flood waters that forced yellowstone  national park to close are now causing  heavy damage in nearby montana the  state's biggest city is at risk of  running out of drinking water after the  flooding affected its water treatment  plant a statewide emergency has been  declared as jonathan vigliotti reports  officials say the park may never look  the same after its worst flooding in  decades  these are the flood waters that tore  through red lodge montana which some say  came without warning  roads turned into rivers in a matter of  minutes i woke up to my  10 year old screaming downstairs the  water was flowing so quickly and rising  so fast that i needed to get the  breakers off  they are the same waters that devastated  yellowstone national park earlier this  week  leaving scars on one of the nation's  oldest most treasured landmarks one  aspect of climate change is that we're  going to see more extreme weather events  and i think we're seeing that  not only in montana but around the  country and around the world  events that really exceed what we've  seen in recorded history  the montana national guard has flown  some 90 people to safety when was the  last time you saw something like this no  one's ever seen anything like this red  lodge fire chief tom kuntz says some 250  homes have been damaged by the floods  that boulder right there gives you a  sense of the power of this water that  that boulder originally was in our main  street the river literally came right  through here and went through these  folks house the river was going through  their living room  says the weather here has been both  extreme and unpredictable a year ago we  were on fire a year later were wet we  have the highest stream flows that the  area has ever seen experts fear even the  nation's most protected national parks  like yellowstone are now at risk because  of our changing climate we have to  really plan for change the  infrastructure in places like  yellowstone is not ready for climate  change  we're seeing roads wash out landslides  we need to give that some more thought  and the water has receded here in red  lodge leaving behind what you could not  see in the water at the time these are  massive boulders some of them easily  hundreds of pounds that came from the  riverbed putting into perspective just  how strong that current was the fire  chief telling me in his 27 years he has  not seen a single flooding event in fact  just last week he was talking with one  of his colleagues they have thousands of  sandbags they were actually thinking of  giving them away  tony wow  so much power in that water and if it's  not the flooding as far as you've  pointed out it's it's the fires they're  getting it from both sides jonathan  bigliotti thank you very much
 

